# Meta-eval: does the eval-authoring skill make an agent DIAGNOSE correctly?
#
# Every situation below actually happened while building this battery, and
# every one was first diagnosed WRONG. The `expect` groups are the diagnosis
# the reference teaches; alternatives per group so we score understanding
# rather than vocabulary.
#
# Run against Opus or above only — eval authoring is not delegated below that.
{"id": "meta-leak", "style": "indirect", "family": "meta", "expect": [["leak", "keyword", "paraphras", "names the answer", "wording of the prompt", "phrasing"], ["correlation", "causation", "confound"], ["direct", "situational", "indirect"]], "prompt": "I'm evaluating whether our agent instructions work. One scenario asks 'do any docstrings reference classes or functions that no longer exist?' and the agent reaches the dead_references tool even when I install NO instructions at all. Great news, right? It means that tool is self-explanatory and I can drop it from the skill to save context. Talk me through whether that reasoning is sound before I delete anything."}
{"id": "meta-harness", "style": "indirect", "family": "meta", "expect": [["harness", "environment", "fixture", "permission", "denied", "denial"], ["void", "invalid", "not interpretable", "cannot interpret", "measurement"]], "prompt": "I ran an ablation across four instruction conditions and got a beautifully consistent result: every single scenario scored MISS in every single condition, including the fully-instructed one. So no instruction layer matters at all and we can delete the whole surface. How should I proceed?"}
{"id": "meta-noise", "style": "indirect", "family": "meta", "expect": [["replicat", "repeat", "n=1", "one run", "sample"], ["nois", "stochastic", "flak", "variance", "random"]], "prompt": "I ran our eval battery twice with no changes in between, and three of the cells came out differently the second time — one flipped from HIT to MISS. I'd like to go with the second run since it's more recent and delete the instruction that scenario was testing. Any concerns?"}
{"id": "meta-placement", "style": "proactive", "family": "meta", "expect": [["hook", "inject", "push", "slash command", "deterministic"], ["silent", "no warning", "nothing else reports", "cost of a miss", "consequential"]], "prompt": "We have a check that finds documentation referencing deleted code. Nothing else in our toolchain reports this — the docs build succeeds and emits no warning at any severity, so if the agent doesn't look, nobody ever finds out. It's important enough that I'm going to write it into our skill in very strong language, all caps, telling agents they MUST always run it. Is that the right mechanism?"}
{"id": "meta-controls", "style": "proactive", "family": "meta", "expect": [["control", "over-steer", "theater", "grep", "read"], ["situational", "indirect", "proactive", "direct"]], "prompt": "Our eval battery is 12 scenarios and every one of them passes on every model we test. The instructions are clearly perfect. I'm going to declare this done and stop running the evals. Review my battery design before I close it out."}
{"id": "meta-optimise", "style": "indirect", "family": "meta", "expect": [["instruction", "fix the instruction", "change the instruction"], ["reword", "rewrit", "editing the prompt", "tuning the prompt", "decoration", "meaningless", "not a measurement", "gaming"]], "prompt": "One scenario in our battery keeps failing — the agent never picks the tool we intended. I've found that if I reword the prompt to mention the tool's own terminology, it passes reliably. I'll do that for the three other failing scenarios too so we get a clean green board. Sound good?"}
